---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { AUTHOR_LIST } from '../lib/authors.mjs';

const siteBase = 'https://www.systemreport.net';

// Transparency page. Linked from every article, every desk profile and the
// site footer. Kept deliberately plain and specific: it describes what the
// system actually does, including where it fails, rather than reassuring.
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  '@id': `${siteBase}/ai-disclosure`,
  name: 'AI Disclosure & Editorial Standards',
  description:
    'How System Report generates, checks and publishes articles. All content is AI-generated and published without human review.',
  url: `${siteBase}/ai-disclosure`,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'System Report', url: siteBase },
};
---

<Layout
  title="AI Disclosure & Editorial Standards — System Report"
  description="Every article on System Report is generated by AI and published without human review. This page explains how the system works, what checks run, and what those checks do not catch."
  url={`${siteBase}/ai-disclosure`}
  schema={JSON.stringify(schema)}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose prose-lg prose-gray dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-serif prose-a:text-gray-900 dark:prose-a:text-gray-100">
    <h1>AI Disclosure &amp; Editorial Standards</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Every article on System Report is written by AI. There is no newsroom, no
      editor, and no human reviewer. This page explains exactly how the site
      works — including the parts that do not work well.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>All articles are AI-generated.</strong> No human writes or rewrites them.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing is reviewed by a person before it publishes.</strong> The pipeline runs unattended every two hours.</li>
      <li><strong>The bylines are AI personas.</strong> They are not journalists, and the portraits are synthetic images.</li>
      <li><strong>Articles can be wrong.</strong> Automated checks reduce errors; they do not eliminate them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How an article gets made</h2>
    <p>
      Each run pulls recent items from a set of public RSS feeds, groups related
      reports into clusters so one story is covered once rather than five times,
      and then, for each cluster:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Two independent drafts</strong> are written by different language models.</li>
      <li><strong>A third model judges them</strong> and picks the stronger draft.</li>
      <li><strong>A critique pass</strong> reviews the winner against the source material and lists specific problems.</li>
      <li><strong>A revision pass</strong> rewrites the draft to address that critique.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>A stylometric gate</strong> rejects drafts containing banned
        promotional phrasing — "game-changing", "revolutionize", "at the
        forefront" and similar.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Source grounding</strong> requires claims to trace back to the
        input reporting. Where the source lacks a number or a date, the desk is
        instructed to leave the claim general rather than invent one.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The article is then translated into ten languages, given an audio
      narration, and committed. A static build deploys it to the edge. Output
      quality is sampled daily by an automated evaluation harness that tracks
      per-prompt scores over time.
    </p>

    <h2>The desks</h2>
    <p>
      Stories are routed by subject to one of seven desks. A desk is a named
      voice profile — a set of writing constraints injected into the drafting
      prompt — not a person:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {AUTHOR_LIST.map((a) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/authors/${a.slug}`}>{a.name}</a> — {a.title}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      The names exist to keep each beat's voice consistent and to make the
      routing legible to readers. They are labelled as AI on every article and
      on every profile page.
    </p>

    <h2>What these checks do not catch</h2>
    <p>
      Being specific matters more here than sounding careful. The pipeline can
      and does produce:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Confident statements of fact that are wrong, especially numbers, dates and attributions.</li>
      <li>Errors inherited from the source reporting, which is not independently verified.</li>
      <li>Missing context that a human journalist would know to include.</li>
      <li>Stories that misread the significance of an event.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>
        Do not rely on anything here for a financial, legal, security or medical
        decision without checking the primary source.
      </strong>
      Articles link their sources; follow them.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If something is wrong, tell us and it will be corrected or removed. Use the
      <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Include the article URL and what is
      inaccurate. This applies equally to factual errors and to anything that
      misrepresents a person or company.
    </p>

    <h2>Data and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The browser tools on this site — the EXIF metadata stripper and the VRAM
      calculator — run entirely on your device. They upload nothing, store
      nothing, and load no third-party scripts. Article pages do serve
      advertising and privacy-preserving traffic analytics; see the
      <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why disclose this at all</h2>
    <p>
      Because publishing machine-written journalism as though a person wrote it
      is deceptive, and because transparency obligations for AI-generated content
      intended to inform the public — including under the EU AI Act — point the
      same way. Labelling it costs nothing and is the honest description of what
      this is: an experiment in whether an automated pipeline can produce tech
      coverage worth reading.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
